Navigation system, sampling ports
=================================

A navigation system split over three partitions that share data, to study CPU,
memory and I/O interference between communicating partitions.

What it tests
-------------

Inter-partition communication through sampling ports: a producer and two
consumers. Compare with NAV_SYS_MONO_QUEUING, which uses queuing ports.

What it does
------------

Three partitions: GPS_P, SPDI_P and ALPR_P, two processes each.

GPS_P generates GPS positions every 2 s and sends them to SPDI_P and ALPR_P
over sampling ports. SPDI_P computes the distance, speed and bearing; ALPR_P
computes the altitude and pressure. In each partition a communication process
and a processing process share data under a semaphore. Windows: GPS_P 500 ms,
SPDI_P 1000 ms, ALPR_P 500 ms.

Expected output
---------------

"NEW POINT GENERATED, <lat>N <lon>E", "Distance ... Speed ... Bearing",
"Altitude ... Pressure".

Build and run
-------------

From the top-level directory (builds every benchmark; QEMU needs only the one):

    make benchmarks                     # x86_64
    make BSP=arm64-qemu benchmarks      # arm64

    qemu-system-x86_64 -vga none -m 1G -nographic \
        -kernel benchmark/NAV_SYS_MONO_SAMPLING/build/x86_64-qemu/pok.elf
    qemu-system-aarch64 -M virt -cpu cortex-a53 -m 1G -nographic \
        -kernel benchmark/NAV_SYS_MONO_SAMPLING/build/arm64-qemu/pok.elf

To build only this benchmark, run "scons bsp=x86_64-qemu" (or bsp=arm64-qemu)
in this directory with POK_PATH set. The benchmark runs forever; quit QEMU with
Ctrl-A X.
